    @supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
      /* The timeline is named on the figure rather than each element calling
         view() for itself: these are SVG children, and one timeline on the
         container is both more reliable and easier to reason about.
         Descendants find it by walking up the tree.

         Ranges use `cover`, not `entry`. The plate is taller than a phone
         viewport, and `entry 100%` is unreachable for an element that never
         fully fits on screen — the animation would stall part-played.

         Every rule below uses the longhands. There is no `animation`
         shorthand anywhere in this block, and there must never be: Lightning
         CSS folds animation-timeline into the shorthand when both are
         present, emitting `animation: … view()`, which no browser parses, so
         the whole declaration is dropped in the production build and nowhere
         else. Landmine 22. */
      .dashboard-plate { view-timeline-name: --plate; }

      /* ⚠️ Every selector below is scoped to .dp-wide. The narrow composition
         reuses the same class names, so an unscoped rule would dim a phone's
         drawing on scroll while spotlighting nothing — the spotlight rects
         exist only in the wide SVG, because the narrow layout stacks its six
         labels under the window rather than framing regions inside it.

         A drawing that dims as you scroll past it, with nothing to explain
         why, is worse than no motion at all on the screen where the drawing
         is already hardest to read. The narrow composition stays static. */
      .dp-wide .dp-window, .dp-wide .dp-side, .dp-wide .dp-head,
      .dp-wide .dp-stat, .dp-wide .dp-chart, .dp-wide .dp-list,
      .dp-wide .co, .dp-wide .dp-spot {
